{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target proposes a causal mechanism (fragmentation error causes checklist panel failure in non-linear tasks) but frames its refutation condition around whether a checklist can be structured as a non-linear graph capturing gestalt patterns. This is a category mismatch: the proposed relation is causal dependence (fragmentation error → failure), but the refutation targets representation capability rather than the causal claim. A checklist being representable as a non-linear graph does not refute that fragmentation error causes failure in the original linear checklist panels — it merely shows an alternative structuring exists. The refutation-if condition is misaligned with the relation it claims to neggate.","counterexample":["A linear checklist with fragmentation error fails a non-linear task; a separately structured graph-checklist succeeds the same task. The causal claim about the linear panel's failure mechanism remains true regardless of whether an alternative structure exists."],"target":"25feee4f5ac5152f56c1a971ea2036556a3f213ce2c8737846369fc05d22e580"}]}